## 5. UX / visual encoding expectations _(semantic — colors come from design.md, not from here)_

- **Unequal station size is the load-bearing argument.** The **CARDIOVASCULAR engine-room must be the dominant compartment** — several times the area of any other station — because "this is the front most likely to end you" is the article's triage. If a reader takes only the biggest box and its red terminus, they should still get "the heart is what kills lifters who looked healthy."
- **The threat→defense grammar must be pre-attentive and consistent.** Every station splits the same way (threat in cautionary ink, defense in instrument ink), so the reader learns it once. The *defense* side should visually outweigh the threat side — protection, not doom, is the subject.
- **The four CV fronts must visibly CONVERGE, not sit in a 2×2 grid.** Their arrows merge into one rupture loop; the compounding is the point. A grid would say "four separate problems," which is the exact misreading the article corrects.
- **Red is rationed to exactly ONE object: the MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION terminus of the rupture loop.** It is the one death event in the article — the thing that actually ends the career. Everything else cautionary — hematocrit, crushed-E2, prolactin/19-nors, RU58841, the blast-and-cruise dependence note, GH glucose — stays **amber/caution** or neutral instrument ink. (Consistent with the whole PE set: ~1 red object, reserved for the one true failure-event.)
- **The super-ancillary cross-ties carry their meaning by FAN-OUT, not by hue.** Each node must visibly send *multiple* lines to *multiple* stations; "double/triple duty" lives in the branching. If color is stripped, the one-to-many routing should still read as "this single drug touches three boxes."
- **The foundations base course must read as load-bearing — the board RESTS on it.** It sits under everything, wider than any station, with the pharmacology explicitly layered above. Don't let it read as a footnote; "foundations are protection, drugs only layer on" is the article's discipline and the PE set's recurring drugs-last rule.
- **Success/green = the defenses holding (in-range, protected); danger = the single MI; amber = every judgement-call caution.** This three-state discipline matches 3.1/3.2 and the PE set — green = the vessel you keep in service, red = the one event you're defending against, amber = the calls.
- **Hierarchy of attention:** headline → the **CV engine-room + its converging loop + the red MI** (hero) → the threat→defense supporting stations → the super-ancillary cross-tie fan-out → the foundations base course → finite-resource grace-note + footer (quiet chrome). Within a station: the organ name loudest, the defense next, the threat mechanism supporting fine print.
- **Restraint + competence = the mood.** A precision protection schematic, not a scare poster or a "before/after autopsy." Dark instrument ground, mono station-labels and tick-marks, ruler-drawn compartments and cross-tie lines, generous negative space. The tone is *a professional keeping a complex machine in service*, not a warning sign. Color rationed to the green defenses, the one red MI, and amber cautions.
- **Saveability:** shape/position carry the meaning (biggest box = gravest front, converging arrows = compounding, station split = threat-vs-defense, cross-tie fan-out = multi-duty, base course = foundations-first) so the board parses on a phone and in grayscale — a reader should still see "the heart is the big one, the fronts compound, each front has a defense, a few drugs do several jobs, foundations hold it up" with color stripped.

